  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nPatchy Dense Fog this Morning and again Tonight: Cloud cover is \nubiquitous across the region early this morning, which has limited\nthe scope and severity of the fog across north-central Wisconsin.\nHowever, cloud cover is still expected to thin out through 5 AM. \nThe decreased cloud cover, combined with radiational cooling and \nlight winds still favors nocturnal ground fog development later \nthis morning across mainly north-central Wisconsin. NBM \nstatistics for visibilities below 1 mile have decreased to 20 to \n40%, mainly across north-central Wisconsin with central Wisconsin \ndown to around 10%. Any fog that forms will rapidly dissipate by \n10 AM as morning solar heating drives boundary layer mixing.\n\nThe threat for fog shifts to the east tonight into Tuesday morning\nas the hi res models and HREF indicate some patchy fog is possible\nacross east-central and far northeast Wisconsin. There is more\nspread in visibilities, with lower chances for visibilities less\nthan 1 mile; however, this will still bear watching as the fog\nforecasting has been tricky the last few days.\n\nProlonged Dry And Mild Early Week Pattern: Deep-layer atmospheric\nsubsidence associated with the persistent surface high pressure\nridge will lock in dry and tranquil weather across the forecast\narea through Tuesday afternoon. Inland high temperatures will\nwarm into the mid-70s today, while light onshore lake breezes \nkeep Lake Michigan shoreline communities slightly cooler in the \nupper 60s. NBM ensemble guidance exhibits high confidence with \ntight 10th to 90th percentile temperature spreads of only 2 to 3 \ndegrees F for daily highs today. \n\nMidweek Frontal System And Widespread Rainfall: Deterministic and\nensemble guidance consensus tracks a slow-moving northern stream\ntrough and advancing surface cold front into the Upper\nMississippi Valley Tuesday night into Wednesday night. Strong \nlow-level moisture transport will elevate rain probabilities to \n60 to 90 percent Tuesday night through Wednesday night. NBM \nprobabilistic guidance indicates a 70 to 90 percent probability \nof 48-hour QPF exceeding 0.50 inches across the region, with a\nsolid 60 to 80 percent of 1 inch or more, and 30 to 60 percent\nchance of 2 inches or more west of the Fox Valley. NBM 50th \npercentile liquid accumulation ranges between 1.50 and 2.6 \ninches along and west of the Fox Valley. SBCAPE values under 300 \nJ/kg and poor shear-moisture overlap will keep thunderstorm \nactivity isolated, mainly south of highway 10, and mitigate any \nsevere weather potential.\n\nLate Week Cooling Trend And Frost Potential: Post-frontal cold\nair advection will usher in a cooler and drier airmass Thursday\nthrough the weekend, dropping daytime highs into the low to \nmid-60s area-wide. As high pressure builds southward from Canada, \nlight winds and clearing skies will promote efficient radiational \ncooling on Friday night. NBM temperature percentiles show \novernight minimum temperatures dropping into the mid to upper 30s \nacross Vilas, Oneida, and Forest counties Friday night. This setup\nyields a 15 to 30 percent probability of frost in sheltered low- \nlying spots across the Northwoods before high pressure begins \nshifting east over the weekend.",
